                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The bottleneck, the engagement slide and the flight risk all trace back to one thing — every pressure routes through an isolated leader with no peer support and nothing delegated. Now you can fix the structure instead of asking her to work even harder.`,
                    coach_r1_effort:        `<strong>"Just work harder" is the trap that built this.</strong> Elena is already at 70+ hours, has cancelled every 1:1, and her wellbeing index is 42. More effort deepens the isolation and degrades exactly the judgment the deadline demands. Effort is not the binding constraint — isolation is.`,
                    coach_r1_team:          `<strong>Careful — blaming the team is the comfortable answer.</strong> These are the same engineers who delivered before the change; the problem is that nothing has been delegated and the leader is the bottleneck. Distrust here becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.`,
                    coach_r1_schedule:      `<strong>The schedule is a symptom, not the cause.</strong> You can re-plan Atlas all you like, but if every decision still queues on one desk it slips again. Fix the decision bottleneck and the isolation behind it, and the schedule becomes manageable.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the isolation as the root cause AND chose to rebuild a confidential support relationship first — a place to be uncertain and pressure-test decisions. That is the lever that links the bottleneck, engagement and wellbeing all at once.`,
                    coach_r1_boardonly:     `<strong>Managing upward matters, but it isn't enough.</strong> A board sponsor holds you accountable; they are not the place to be uncertain at 11pm before a hard call. Without a confidential peer or coach, Elena keeps deciding alone.`,
                    coach_r1_clientonly:    `<strong>The client is real, but it isn't the first relationship to rebuild.</strong> Pleasing the client while the internal structure burns leaves Elena more isolated and the team more disengaged. Build the support that protects her judgment first.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's how you get to 80 hours and a wellbeing index of 30.</strong> "Just push harder on the queue" is precisely the hero trap the promotion created. There absolutely is a relationship to rebuild — start with a confidential place to be uncertain.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>Low candor here means you'll prescribe "more effort" and call it a plan.</strong> If the team won't name the isolation honestly, it will keep treating symptoms. Name the real constraint or you'll repeat the pattern.`,
                    coach_r2_coachstaff:    `<strong>A coach plus a chief of staff is the strongest scaffolding.</strong> The coach protects her judgment under pressure; the chief of staff runs delivery cadence so the queue stops routing through her. It costs hours now and buys capacity for the rest of the simulation.`,
                    coach_r2_peerboard:     `<strong>A confidential peer-MD board is a smart, low-cost move.</strong> Other MDs who have sat in this chair give Elena a place to be uncertain and pressure-test decisions for very little budget. Pair it with delegation and you have real support architecture.`,
                    coach_r2_staffonly:     `<strong>A chief of staff helps the bottleneck — but it isn't a place to be uncertain.</strong> Process support without a confidential relationship leaves Elena still deciding alone at the hardest moments. The thing that protects judgment is the relationship, not just the process.`,
                    coach_r2_nosupport:     `<strong>Skipping support to "reinvest in the project" is the coach-as-luxury error.</strong> The support relationship is most needed when time is scarcest — by Round 3 she will be deciding alone again and decision quality will fall. What did saving those hours cost?`,
                    coach_r2_rival:         `<strong>Trusting the rival with a real mandate is the bold, correct move.</strong> Giving the passed-over lead the legacy integration with clear decision rights repairs the relationship, keeps the institutional knowledge, and clears part of the queue. Delegation is an act of trust that develops the team.`,
                    coach_r2_lieutenants:   `<strong>Delegating to trusted lieutenants clears the queue — but you routed around the rival.</strong> That avoids the hard conversation now and leaves the knowledge-loss risk live. Watch what happens when he escalates next week.`,
                    coach_r2_delegateall:   `<strong>Handing authority to everyone at once is speed without safety.</strong> Without escalation thresholds you've turned a bottleneck into a scatter of unowned decisions. Delegation needs structure, or errors slip through.`,
                    coach_r2_hold:          `<strong>Keeping all 23 decisions is the hero trap, deepened.</strong> "Only Elena can be trusted" guarantees the bottleneck grows and her wellbeing collapses. The job now is to make herself less necessary to every decision, not more.`,
                    coach_r2_noprotocol:    `<strong>No protocol means you can't safely delegate at all.</strong> Decision rights and escalation thresholds are what turn delegation into capacity rather than chaos. Without them you keep everything — or you abdicate.`,
                    coach_r2_abdication:    `<strong>Delegation without decision rights is abdication.</strong> You handed out real authority with no escalation thresholds — predictably, a costly decision went the wrong way. The guardrails are what make trust safe. Add the thresholds.`,
                    coach_r2_norecovery:    `<strong>Zero recovery is a false economy.</strong> Chronic 70-hour weeks degrade the judgment a high-stakes deadline most needs. A protected recovery block is an input to decision quality — protect a minimum, even now.`,
                    coach_r3_scope:         `<strong>Renegotiating scope to ship the critical core is the defensible recovery path.</strong> Phasing the rest protects the 30 November date on what actually matters, keeps the renewal alive, and is a far better story for the board than a silent slip.`,
                    coach_r3_contractors:   `<strong>Contractors can help — but pushing the existing team harder is the risk.</strong> The four flight-risk engineers are exactly who you can't afford to lose. Adding bodies to a late critical path without protecting the team can make Atlas later, not sooner.`,
                    coach_r3_date:          `<strong>Quietly planning to slip and absorb the penalty is the worst of both.</strong> You give up the €4M renewal and €1.8M penalty without even trying the recovery, and the board hears it too late. Choose a defensible path and own it.`,
                    coach_r3_engage:        `<strong>Engaging the rival directly is a delivery decision, not a soft one.</strong> Confirming his mandate and making him part of the solution keeps the legacy integration knowledge in the building and turns a threat into a contributor.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>You routed around the passed-over lead — and he resigned this week.</strong> He took the only person who understands the legacy integration, and Atlas just lost two more weeks. Was excluding him a wellbeing decision or an avoidance decision?`,
                    coach_r3_push:          `<strong>Calling his bluff under pressure rarely ends well.</strong> Pressuring the rival to fall in line or leave usually loses both the person and the knowledge — and the rest of the team is watching how their MD treats dissent.`,
                    coach_r3_amber:         `<strong>Honest amber plus a credible recovery plan is exactly right.</strong> Telling the board Atlas is at risk AND showing the phased-scope recovery builds the credibility to ask for help or time. Calibrated candor is strength, not weakness, for a new leader being tested.`,
                    coach_r3_green:         `<strong>You told the board Atlas is green.</strong> In ten days it slips and you have no credibility left to ask for help or time. The board now doubts both the project and the MD. When is honest amber stronger than confident green? Now.`,
                    coach_r3_silent:        `<strong>Saying as little as possible reads as avoidance.</strong> A new MD who goes quiet under a credibility-defining checkpoint loses the board's trust by default. Silence is not a posture — it is a missed chance to build credibility.`,
                    coach_r3_burnout:       `<strong>You cancelled recovery and pushed the team through the weekend.</strong> The dashboard blipped green — then engagement fell, senior engineers walked, and the work they left behind put Atlas back at risk. You spent the people to make the date. Did you actually de-risk it?`,
                    coach_r4_institutionalise: `<strong>Institutionalising the model is what makes this last.</strong> A fixed cadence, kept delegated authorities, a retained coach or peer board and protected recovery turn a crisis response into a sustainable way of leading. This is the difference between surviving and relapsing.`,
                    coach_r4_partial:       `<strong>Dropping the coach and peer board "now the crunch is over" is the classic relapse.</strong> The support was most valuable under pressure — and the next pressure is always coming. Keep the relationships that protect your judgment.`,
                    coach_r4_revert:        `<strong>Reverting to deciding alone undoes everything you built.</strong> "The support was just for the crisis" is exactly how a leader slides back into the lonely chair. The isolation pattern returns, and so does the bottleneck.`,
                    coach_r4_reposition:    `<strong>Retaining and repositioning the rival closes the loop.</strong> Giving him the platform architecture with a clear growth path keeps the knowledge, models that dissent is welcome, and finally puts the relationship to work as a delivery asset.`,
                    coach_r4_release:       `<strong>A clean, respectful exit is defensible if it's genuine.</strong> Managed well with proper knowledge transfer it beats a sidelined, resentful lead — though repairing and repositioning would have kept more value in the building.`,
                    coach_r4_sideline:      `<strong>Leaving the rival sidelined and hoping is not a resolution.</strong> An ignored, resentful lead is a knowledge-loss risk and a signal to the whole team. Hope is not a leadership strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_hero:          `<strong>"I personally drove this through" is the wrong story to tell.</strong> Taking all the credit signals you are still the single point of failure — exactly the pattern the board promoted you out of. Sustainable leadership shares the win and shows the structure behind it.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>Vague reassurance dodges the question the sponsor actually asked.</strong> "Is the MD sustainable?" deserves a real answer with named owners and a rhythm — high-level hand-waving just defers the doubt to the next checkpoint.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready answer.</strong> You showed Atlas status honestly, the support model, named owners and a sustainable rhythm — and made the case that lasting leadership beats heroics. The sponsor's real question — is the MD sustainable? — you answered yes, with evidence.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
